TypeScript Lesson 15: Inheritance (extends & super) 🏗️
Aaj hum Inheritance seekhenge. Iska use tab hota hai jab ek class doosri class ke properties aur methods reuse karna chahti hai.
1. Inheritance kya hota hai?
Maan lo ek Animal class hai. Usmein animals ka naam aur sound karne ka method hai.
Ab Dog aur Cat classes ko bhi ye features chahiye. Kya humein same code baar-baar likhna padega? Nahi! Hum inheritance use kar sakte hain.





















2. extends ka use
class Animal {
    constructor(public name: string) {}

    eat(): void {
        console.log(this.name + " is eating");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    bark(): void {
        console.log(this.name + " says Woof!");
    }
}

const dog = new Dog("Tommy");

dog.eat();
dog.bark();


Output:
Tommy is eating
Tommy says Woof!


Samjho:
- Animal = parent/base class.
- Dog = child/derived class.
- extends Animal = Dog, Animal ke features inherit karta hai.
- Dog apna extra method bark() bhi bana sakta hai.
Memory trick: extends = parent class ke features reuse karna.
3. super() kya hota hai?
Jab child class apna constructor define karti hai, to parent class ka constructor call karne ke liye super() use karna hota hai.
class Animal {
    constructor(public name: string) {}
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    constructor(name: string, public breed: string) {
        super(name);
    }
}

const dog = new Dog("Tommy", "Labrador");

console.log(dog.name);
console.log(dog.breed);


Output:
Tommy
Labrador


super(name) parent Animal class ke constructor ko name deta hai.
Important: Derived class ke constructor mein this use karne se pehle super() call karna zaroori hai.
4. Method overriding
Child class parent ke method ko apne hisaab se implement kar sakti hai. Isko method overriding kehte hain.
class Animal {
    makeSound(): void {
        console.log("Animal makes a sound");
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    override makeSound(): void {
        console.log("Dog barks");
    }
}

const animal = new Animal();
const dog = new Dog();

animal.makeSound();
dog.makeSound();


Output:
Animal makes a sound
Dog barks


override TypeScript ko batata hai ki child class jaan-bujhkar parent ke method ko replace kar rahi hai.
5. Real-world example: Employees
class Employee {
    constructor(
        public name: string,
        public salary: number
    ) {}

    showDetails(): void {
        console.log(this.name + " earns " + this.salary);
    }
}

class Manager extends Employee {
    constructor(
        name: string,
        salary: number,
        public teamSize: number
    ) {
        super(name, salary);
    }
}

const manager = new Manager("Rahul", 60000, 5);

manager.showDetails();
console.log(manager.teamSize);


Output:
Rahul earns 60000
5


Manager ko Employee ka name, salary aur showDetails() method reuse karne ko milta hai.

